      "Collections vary in wear layer and thickness",
      "Mid-range price, not a budget option"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-supported, pet-minded vinyl plank that is easy to buy and install.",
    "description": "Pergo, better known for laminate, sells Pergo Extreme as its rigid-core vinyl line, marketed around resistance to pet accidents, scratches and spills. Most collections include an attached pad, and Pergo's click system is designed to be forgiving for DIY installers, with owners commonly reporting that rows lock together cleanly. It is widely stocked at Lowe's and other retailers, which makes it easy to match later for repairs. The range spans several tiers, so compare the wear layer and thickness of each collection: pick a thicker-wear-layer option for kitchens and homes with dogs, and a basic tier for bedrooms. As with any vinyl, the plank itself is waterproof but the installed floor is not a sealed pan, so pet accidents should still be cleaned promptly and the perimeter sealed in wet rooms. Pricing generally sits in the mid-range, roughly $3 to $5 per square foot at the time of writing."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Vinyl Plank Flooring Picks",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which vinyl plank line suits most homes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, a rigid-core plank with a 12 to 20 mil wear layer and an attached pad is the sweet spot. COREtec Plus Premium is the comfort-focused premium pick, Shaw Floorté Pro and Mohawk SolidTech Plus are durable mid-range choices, and Lifeproof or NuCore offer good value if you pick a mid-tier collection rather than the cheapest line."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is SPC or WPC better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither is better everywhere. SPC is denser and resists dents from appliances, furniture and rolling chairs, and it is more stable in temperature swings. WPC is thicker, softer and quieter underfoot, which makes it more pleasant in bedrooms and family rooms. Choose SPC for kitchens, basements and rentals, and WPC where comfort matters most."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How thick should the wear layer be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use 6 to 8 mil only for closets and guest rooms, 12 mil for most bedrooms and living spaces, and 20 mil for kitchens, entries, hallways and homes with large dogs. Above 20 mil is aimed at commercial spaces. The wear layer, not total plank thickness, decides how long the surface keeps looking new."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you lay vinyl plank on a concrete slab?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Vinyl is among the most forgiving floors for slabs, including basements. Test for moisture first, fill dips and grind high spots to the flatness tolerance, and lay the vapor barrier the manufacturer calls for, usually 6 mil poly with taped seams. A slab that is visibly wet needs the water problem fixed before any floor goes down."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does vinyl plank dent or scratch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can. Narrow, heavy loads dent softer cores, and grit dragged across the floor scratches the wear layer. Use wide coasters under appliances and pianos, felt pads under every chair leg, a mat under rolling chairs and walk-off mats at doors. A 20 mil wear layer and an SPC core resist both problems best."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you put vinyl plank in a bathroom?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The planks will not swell from water, which makes vinyl a good bathroom floor. Leave the expansion gap, seal the perimeter at the tub, shower and toilet with 100 percent silicone, and do not bolt the toilet through the floor if the manufacturer prohibits it. Wipe standing water quickly so none reaches the subfloor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does vinyl plank flooring last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A quality plank installed over a flat, dry subfloor commonly lasts 15 to 25 years in residential use, and longer in low-traffic rooms. Thin wear layers in busy kitchens and entries wear sooner, sometimes in 5 to 10 years. Good mats, felt pads and gentle cleaning stretch the life of any vinyl floor considerably."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Flooring, Grooming & Misc",
    tags: [
      "best vinyl plank flooring",
      "luxury vinyl plank",
      "spc vs wpc",
      "vinyl plank wear layer",
      "waterproof flooring",
      "lvp installation"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Vinyl Plank Floors</span> for Real Homes
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A good vinyl plank floor is decided by three numbers on the spec sheet and one job you do before the first plank goes down. Here is how the leading lines compare, and which one fits your room.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#coretec-plus-premium"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Compare All Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#spec-sheet" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Reading the Spec Sheet</a>
          <a href="#core-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. SPC, WPC and Flexible</a>
          <a href="#room-by-room" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Choosing by Room</a>
          <a href="#subfloor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Subfloor Prep</a>
          <a href="#install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Installing a Click Floor</a>
          <a href="#water" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What Waterproof Really Covers</a>
          <a href="#vs-laminate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Vinyl Plank or Laminate</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. What It Costs</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Care and Repairs</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Luxury vinyl plank sells on photos of wood grain, but the photo is the least important layer. What decides whether the floor still looks good in ten years is the clear wear layer on top, the rigid core underneath, and how flat the subfloor was on installation day. Get those right and a mid-priced plank will outlast a pricier one laid carelessly. The picks below are sorted by the job they suit, not by how expensive they are.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Vinyl Plank Floors, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are ranked on published wear-layer thickness, core construction, attached underlayment, residential and commercial warranty terms, availability, and recurring patterns in owner feedback about denting, gapping and click-lock durability. No floor was installed or trialed for this list; specifications come from manufacturer data sheets and retailer listings, and they vary by collection, so confirm the exact line before you order.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="spec-sheet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Read the Spec Sheet, Not the Box Front
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every vinyl plank box advertises thickness in millimeters, and that number is mostly marketing. A 7 mm plank and a 5 mm plank can wear identically if they carry the same top layer. The figure that predicts scratch and scuff life is the <strong>wear layer</strong>, measured in mils (thousandths of an inch). It is the transparent urethane-and-vinyl skin that sits over the printed image. Once traffic grinds through it, the print shows wear and nothing short of replacing the plank fixes it.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a working rule, 6 to 8 mil is fine for a guest bedroom or closet, 12 mil is the floor for most living spaces, and 20 mil is what you want for kitchens, entries, homes with large dogs and light commercial rooms. Anything above 20 mil is aimed at retail and offices. The step from 12 to 20 mil usually adds well under a dollar per square foot, and in a busy room it is the cheapest durability you can buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second number is the core. Rigid stone-plastic composite cores resist dents and hide subfloor bumps better than flexible vinyl. Look for the core thickness separately from the total: a plank listed at 8 mm that includes a 1.5 mm attached pad has a 6.5 mm board. A thicker rigid core bridges minor dips better, though it does not excuse a subfloor that is out of flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, check the attached underlayment, if any, and the warranty wording. Attached cork or IXPE foam adds comfort and sound control and usually means you should not add a separate pad. Warranties that read lifetime residential often exclude scratches, dents, sun fading and water damage from below, so the fine print matters more than the headline. A 10- or 15-year light commercial warranty is a better tell of real toughness than any lifetime residential promise.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Spec</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to look for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it matters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wear layer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 mil minimum, 20 mil for busy rooms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sets scratch and scuff life</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
