        <div id="core-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. SPC, WPC and Flexible Vinyl: Which Core Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>SPC</strong>, stone plastic composite, blends powdered limestone with PVC into a dense board. It is heavy, hard and very dimensionally stable, so it shrugs off rolling chairs and furniture legs better than other cores and moves little with temperature swings. The trade-off is feel: SPC is firm and can sound clicky without a good pad. It is the default choice for most kitchens, basements and rental units, and it dominates the budget and mid-range market.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>WPC</strong>, originally wood plastic composite, uses a foamed PVC core that is thicker and lighter. It feels softer and warmer underfoot and is quieter, which is why many premium lines use it. Because the core is foamed, it is more prone to denting under concentrated loads like a loaded piano bench, a refrigerator on narrow feet or a desk chair without a mat. Use felt pads and wide furniture cups and that rarely becomes a problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flexible or glue-down vinyl plank is the older format. It is thin, 2 to 3 mm, and either glues to the subfloor or uses a loose-lay backing. It telegraphs every bump, so it needs a near-perfect substrate, but it handles commercial traffic well, works on stairs and in rooms that see large temperature swings, and is easy to replace plank by plank. Most homeowners doing their own install are better served by a click-lock rigid core.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not chase hybrid labels. Some brands now sell rigid cores with mineral or magnesium content and give them new names. What matters is still the same: dent resistance under load, the wear layer, and how the locking joint holds up after years of expansion and contraction. If two products have the same wear layer and similar weight per carton, they will usually behave similarly no matter what the core is called.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Heavy furniture and pets point to SPC; comfort and quiet point to WPC.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the room has a refrigerator, a piano, rolling chairs or a large dog, start with SPC. If it is a bedroom or family room where people stand and walk barefoot, WPC with attached cork feels noticeably better.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="room-by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing by Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Kitchens</strong> take dropped pans, chair scrapes and standing water from a leaking dishwasher. Choose an SPC core with at least a 20 mil wear layer and a tight, angle-angle lock. Run the floor under the dishwasher opening before installing the appliance if the manufacturer allows it, and leave the specified gap at cabinets. Do not lock cabinets down on top of a floating floor, because that pins it and can cause buckling elsewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Basements</strong> are where vinyl plank earns its reputation. It tolerates concrete slabs that are slightly damp, provided you use the vapor barrier the manufacturer calls for. Test the slab first by taping an 18-inch square of plastic down for 24 to 48 hours; condensation underneath means you have a moisture problem to solve before any floor goes down. A <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a> that holds the room under about 55 percent humidity protects both the floor and anything stored on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bathrooms</strong> suit vinyl plank well because the planks themselves do not swell. Seal the perimeter at the tub and toilet with a flexible silicone so water cannot get under the floor and sit on the subfloor. Most manufacturers prohibit installing under a toilet with the flange bolted through the plank, so cut neatly around the flange and caulk.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bedrooms and living rooms</strong> are the place to save. A 12 mil wear layer is enough in low-traffic rooms, and WPC or a cork-backed SPC gives a quieter, warmer feel. Sunrooms and rooms with large south-facing glass need a product rated for direct sun and the temperature range it will see; check the data sheet for a maximum temperature, because some rigid cores expand enough in hot sun to peak at the joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over radiant floor heat, confirm the plank is approved for it and keep the surface below the manufacturer's limit, often around 85 degrees Fahrenheit. In three-season rooms and unheated cabins, look for a product rated for unconditioned spaces, as many are not, and installing one there can void the warranty. In basements, also look at what stands on the slab: rusty steel support columns and water heater stands should be treated before the new floor goes in around them, and our guide to <a href="/rust-converter-vs-rust-remover/">rust converter vs rust remover</a> explains which approach suits a fixed column.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subfloor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Subfloor Prep Decides the Result
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most failed vinyl plank floors fail at the joints, and most joint failures start with a subfloor that is not flat. Manufacturers typically specify flatness of three-sixteenths of an inch over 10 feet, or an eighth of an inch over 6 feet. Lay a straight 6-foot level or a long aluminum straightedge in several directions across the room and slide a shim under any gaps. Hollows deeper than the tolerance need filling, and humps need grinding or sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            On concrete, fill low spots with a cement-based patch or self-leveling underlayment and grind high spots with a cup wheel. On plywood or OSB, screw down loose panels to stop squeaks, sand proud seams and fill dips with a floor patch rated for wood. Never install a floating floor over carpet, cushioned vinyl or anything soft, because the locking joints flex with every step and eventually separate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Old sheet vinyl or tile that is firmly bonded and flat is usually an acceptable base, but many pre-1980s floors and their adhesives contain asbestos. Do not sand, scrape or grind old resilient flooring without testing it. Covering it with a floating floor is often the safest option, and it is allowed by most manufacturers if the existing floor is a single, well-adhered layer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acclimate the cartons as the instructions say. Rigid cores need less time than wood, but most makers still ask for the boxes to sit flat in the room at living temperature for 24 to 48 hours. Keep the house heated or cooled during installation and afterward, since a floating floor installed in a cold, closed-up house will expand once the heat comes back on.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
