        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Installing a Click-Lock Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Click-lock vinyl plank is among the most approachable floors for a first-time installer. The tools are short: a utility knife with plenty of blades, a speed square, a tapping block and pull bar made for the product, spacers, a rubber mallet, and a jamb saw for undercutting door casings. Rigid cores score and snap; you only need a saw for notches and rips, and a fine-tooth jigsaw or oscillating tool handles those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the layout before you click anything. Measure the room width and divide by the plank width so the last row is not a sliver; if it would be under about 2 inches, rip the first row narrower so both edges match. Stagger end joints by at least 6 to 8 inches, avoid repeating patterns, and pull from three or four cartons at once so color and grain variation spread evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the expansion gap all the way around, usually about a quarter inch, including at door jambs, pipes, cabinet toe kicks and stair noses. Baseboard or quarter-round covers the gap. Long continuous runs and large open plans often need a transition strip at doorways or every 40 to 50 feet; check your product's maximum run length, because exceeding it is one of the most common causes of peaking joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work slowly at the first two rows, because everything else follows them. Lock the long side at a low angle and drop the short end into place, tapping lightly with the block. Too much force cracks the tongue on rigid cores, and a cracked lock is a weak spot forever. If a joint will not close, unclick it and look for debris rather than hammering harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave rooms with heavy appliances for last, and slide appliances back on plywood or a hard runner so feet do not gouge the new surface. Install felt pads under every furniture leg before moving it back in. The floor is usable as soon as it is laid, which is one of the main advantages over glue-down products and site-finished wood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Waterproof Really Covers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Waterproof on a vinyl plank box means the plank itself will not swell or delaminate if water sits on it. It does not mean the installed floor is a pan. Water that runs into the expansion gap at the walls or seeps through joints can reach the subfloor and stay there, trapped under a plastic floor that will not let it dry. On plywood, that leads to swelling, odor and mold well before the floor itself shows damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat spills promptly and seal the perimeter in wet rooms. In bathrooms and laundry rooms, run a bead of 100 percent silicone at the tub, shower and toilet, and consider it at the walls too if the manufacturer permits. Many warranties cover topical spills only and exclude flooding, hydrostatic pressure and water coming up from below, so a plumbing leak or a basement seep is your problem, not theirs.
          </p>

          <p>
            After a flood, floating vinyl plank has one real advantage: you can unclick and pull it up, dry the subfloor with fans and a dehumidifier, and in many cases reinstall the same planks. Keep a spare carton for that day. A wet-dry <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> pulls standing water out of the gap faster than towels.
          </p>

          <p>
            On slabs, the vapor barrier is the defense against moisture from below. Use the poly sheet or barrier the manufacturer specifies, overlap seams by 6 to 8 inches and tape them, and turn it up the wall slightly under the baseboard. If your slab is visibly damp, dark in patches or white with efflorescence, fix the water source first, because no floor survives a wet slab for long.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-laminate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Vinyl Plank or Laminate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate has come a long way. Modern water-resistant lines with sealed edges and wax-treated cores tolerate spills for many hours, and laminate's melamine wear surface resists scratches better than most vinyl at the same price. It also feels more like wood: harder, warmer-sounding and with deeper embossing. For a dry living room or bedroom with pets, a good laminate is often the more scratch-resistant choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinyl wins wherever water is a real risk or the slab is below grade. Its core does not swell no matter how long water sits, it is softer and quieter on the ear, and it is more forgiving of small subfloor flaws. Basements, full bathrooms, laundry rooms, mudrooms and rentals tend to favor vinyl. If you are weighing the two, our <a href="/best-laminate-flooring/">laminate flooring guide</a> covers the AC ratings and sealed-edge lines that close the gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many homes do best with both. Run laminate through the bedrooms and living spaces, and vinyl plank in kitchens, baths and the basement. Matching colors across product types is easier than it used to be, and a T-molding at the transition is barely noticed. Avoid mixing two floors in one open space, because slight differences in sheen and thickness stand out in raking light.
          </p>

