        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Vinyl Plank Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, budget rigid-core planks start around $1.50 to $2.50 per square foot, mid-range lines with a 12 to 20 mil wear layer and attached pad run roughly $2.50 to $4, and premium brands with thick wear layers, cork backing and long commercial warranties sit around $4 to $7. Prices swing with sales, so it is worth waiting for a promotion on a big order.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add 7 to 10 percent for waste on straight rooms and 12 to 15 percent for rooms with many corners, closets or a diagonal layout. Budget for underlayment only if the product has no attached pad, plus transition strips, quarter-round, a vapor barrier on concrete and floor patch. For a 400 square-foot room those extras commonly add $200 to $500.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional installation of click-lock vinyl runs roughly $2 to $5 per square foot in most markets, more if the installer must remove old flooring, level the subfloor or work around stairs. Stairs are priced per step and are much slower to do well. Doing the install yourself saves the most money in big open rooms with simple layouts and the least in small, chopped-up spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where to spend: the wear layer and subfloor prep. Where to save: total plank thickness, fancy bevel names and a lifetime residential warranty that excludes nearly everything. A 12 mil plank installed flat beats a 22 mil plank laid over a wavy floor every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Care, Cleaning and Repairs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grit is the enemy of the wear layer. Put mats inside and outside every exterior door, sweep or vacuum with a hard-floor head rather than a beater bar, and damp-mop with a cleaner made for vinyl or plain warm water. Avoid oil soaps, wax, paste polish and steam mops, which can leave a film or push heat and moisture into the joints. Most manufacturers list approved cleaners in their care guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Felt pads under every chair and table leg matter more than any cleaner. Replace them when they pick up grit. Use a clear chair mat under rolling office chairs, because hard casters will eventually scuff even a 20 mil surface. For refrigerators and pianos, wide non-staining furniture cups spread the load and prevent dents in softer cores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rubber-backed rugs and mats can stain vinyl yellow over time through a chemical reaction. Use mats labeled safe for vinyl floors, or a rug pad made of felt or natural rubber. Close blinds on long, sunny afternoons in rooms with big windows, since prolonged sun can fade some prints and heat the planks enough to expand.
          </p>

          <p>
            A damaged plank near a wall is easy to replace: remove the baseboard, unclick rows back to the damaged one, swap it and click everything back. In the middle of a room, installers cut out the damaged plank, trim the locking edges off the replacement, and glue it in place. Either way, the spare carton you kept at installation will match far better than a new box from a different production run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on total thickness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thick plank with a thin wear layer scuffs as quickly as a thin one. Compare wear layer in mils first, then core type, then thickness. For kitchens, entries and homes with big dogs, 20 mil is the target, and the price step from 12 mil is usually small compared with replacing a worn floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the flatness check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid cores hide small bumps, not dips. If the subfloor is out more than the manufacturer allows, joints flex, click locks wear and gaps appear within a year or two. Check with a long straightedge, fill low spots, grind high spots, and only then start laying planks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pinning the floating floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kitchen islands, cabinets and heavy built-ins screwed through a floating floor stop it from moving, and the stress shows up as peaked joints elsewhere. Install cabinets first and run the floor to them with a gap, or follow the manufacturer's rules for heavy fixed loads.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a second pad under attached underlayment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Planks with attached IXPE or cork are designed to sit directly on the subfloor or vapor barrier. A second foam layer makes the floor too soft, stressing the locking joints until they break. Use only a thin poly vapor barrier on slabs unless the instructions say otherwise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting waterproof to protect the subfloor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The planks will survive a spill; the plywood under them may not. Water that gets into the perimeter gap or joints can sit for weeks. Caulk wet-room perimeters with silicone, mop up spills fast and fix leaks before they soak the subfloor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which vinyl plank line suits most homes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, a rigid-core plank with a 12 to 20 mil wear layer and an attached pad is the sweet spot. COREtec Plus Premium is the comfort-focused premium pick, Shaw Floorté Pro and Mohawk SolidTech Plus are durable mid-range choices, and Lifeproof or NuCore offer good value if you pick a mid-tier collection rather than the cheapest line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is SPC or WPC better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better everywhere. SPC is denser and resists dents from appliances, furniture and rolling chairs, and it is more stable in temperature swings. WPC is thicker, softer and quieter underfoot, which makes it more pleasant in bedrooms and family rooms. Choose SPC for kitchens, basements and rentals, and WPC where comfort matters most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should the wear layer be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use 6 to 8 mil only for closets and guest rooms, 12 mil for most bedrooms and living spaces, and 20 mil for kitchens, entries, hallways and homes with large dogs. Above 20 mil is aimed at commercial spaces. The wear layer, not total plank thickness, decides how long the surface keeps looking new.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you lay vinyl plank on a concrete slab?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Vinyl is among the most forgiving floors for slabs, including basements. Test for moisture first, fill dips and grind high spots to the flatness tolerance, and lay the vapor barrier the manufacturer calls for, usually 6 mil poly with taped seams. A slab that is visibly wet needs the water problem fixed before any floor goes down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinyl plank dent or scratch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Narrow, heavy loads dent softer cores, and grit dragged across the floor scratches the wear layer. Use wide coasters under appliances and pianos, felt pads under every chair leg, a mat under rolling chairs and walk-off mats at doors. A 20 mil wear layer and an SPC core resist both problems best.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put vinyl plank in a bathroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The planks will not swell from water, which makes vinyl a good bathroom floor. Leave the expansion gap, seal the perimeter at the tub, shower and toilet with 100 percent silicone, and do not bolt the toilet through the floor if the manufacturer prohibits it. Wipe standing water quickly so none reaches the subfloor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does vinyl plank flooring last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality plank installed over a flat, dry subfloor commonly lasts 15 to 25 years in residential use, and longer in low-traffic rooms. Thin wear layers in busy kitchens and entries wear sooner, sometimes in 5 to 10 years. Good mats, felt pads and gentle cleaning stretch the life of any vinyl floor considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

